1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,936
Hello, I'm Camille.

2
00:00:02,286 --> 00:00:07,958
Today, I'm talking to businesses: fake bank details fraud, and how to protect yourself.

3
00:00:08,658 --> 00:00:12,090
An email arrives, apparently from one of your regular suppliers.

4
00:00:12,440 --> 00:00:16,560
It announces a change of bank, and gives new bank details.

5
00:00:16,910 --> 00:00:20,478
The address looks like the real one. And you're asked to be quick.

6
00:00:20,828 --> 00:00:27,260
The next transfers then go to the scammer's account. Often, the supplier's mailbox has been hacked.

7
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:29,968
A variant: CEO fraud.

8
00:00:30,318 --> 00:00:37,174
A fake executive urgently requests an exceptional, confidential transfer, outside the usual procedures.

9
00:00:37,874 --> 00:00:39,882
Here is the procedure to put in place.

10
00:00:40,232 --> 00:00:43,792
One: every change of bank details is confirmed by phone.

11
00:00:44,142 --> 00:00:48,886
Two: call the supplier's number you already know, never the one given in the message.

12
00:00:49,236 --> 00:00:52,380
Three: unusual transfers are approved by two people.

13
00:00:52,730 --> 00:00:56,594
Four: an urgent, confidential request always triggers a check.

14
00:00:57,294 --> 00:01:01,366
If a fraudulent transfer has already gone, every minute counts.

15
00:01:01,716 --> 00:01:04,140
Call your bank immediately to try to recall it.

16
00:01:04,490 --> 00:01:06,706
Alert the supplier and your management.

17
00:01:07,056 --> 00:01:08,808
Then report it to the police.

18
00:01:09,508 --> 00:01:11,420
Two safeguards are better than one.

19
00:01:11,770 --> 00:01:15,610
That was Camille, for Prometis. See you soon!
